## Symptom

A BOM export Method slowed down as the customer's data grew. Profiling showed thousands of tiny database round trips, one per Part.

```csharp
foreach (string id in partIds)
{
    Item part = inn.getItemById("Part", id); // one request per Part
    // ...
}
```

## Fix

Ask for many items in one request with the `idlist` attribute, and only select the properties you need:

```csharp
const int batchSize = 500;
for (int i = 0; i < partIds.Count; i += batchSize)
{
    string ids = string.Join(",", partIds.Skip(i).Take(batchSize));

    Item query = inn.newItem("Part", "get");
    query.setAttribute("idlist", ids);
    query.setAttribute("select", "item_number,name,state,major_rev");
    Item parts = query.apply();

    if (parts.isError()) throw new Exception(parts.getErrorString());
    for (int j = 0; j < parts.getItemCount(); j++)
    {
        Item part = parts.getItemByIndex(j);
        // ...
    }
}
```

## Why batches of 500

A single huge `idlist` produces a very long `IN (...)` clause. Batching keeps each query reasonable and memory use predictable.

## Notes

The `select` attribute matters as much as batching: without it, every property is loaded, including long text fields.

## Symptom

Saving a Part became very slow and eventually failed with a stack overflow or timeout. The server log showed the same Method running over and over.

## Root cause

The Method ran **onAfterUpdate** and issued its own `edit` on the same Part to store a calculated value. That edit is also an update, so it triggered the event again, which started another edit, and so on.

```csharp
// onAfterUpdate: don't do this
Item edit = inn.newItem("Part", "edit");
edit.setID(this.getID());
edit.setProperty("cn_weight_total", total.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
edit.apply(); // fires onAfterUpdate again
```

## Fix

Calculate the value **before** the save and set it on the incoming item. No second request, no recursion:

```csharp
// onBeforeUpdate (and onBeforeAdd)
decimal total = CalculateWeight(this);
this.setProperty("cn_weight_total", total.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
return this;
```

## Notes

- Use `CultureInfo.InvariantCulture` for numbers. A Dutch or German server locale writes `12,5` instead of `12.5`.
- If you really must update a *different* item from an after-event, guard against re-entry and document why.
